<?xml version="1.0"?>
<!--
  ClickHouse MEMORY-ACCOUNTING drop-in. Mounted at
  /etc/clickhouse-server/config.d/memory.xml so it overlays the packaged config.

  THE PROBLEM THIS FIXES

  ClickHouse refuses work when its global memory tracker passes
  max_server_memory_usage. That tracker is a counter — allocations add, frees
  subtract — and any accounting asymmetry stays in it for good: nothing but a
  restart brings it back down. Measured on both deployments (2026-09-29,
  ClickHouse 24.10.2.80, the version the old `24-alpine` tag is frozen at):

      host   tracker says   real resident   "Memory limit exceeded" errors
      .71     486.69 GiB       904 MiB             120,456
      .45     275.21 GiB      1.05 GiB              67,871

  Every refusal read "would use 5.40 GiB … current RSS 1.06 GiB": the server
  turned away background merges (one retried 5,756 times), its own system-log
  flushes, and 970 queries — including a traffic_buckets insert — while using a
  fifth of its limit. Under load it happens in bursts (soak tests, benchmarks);
  small inserts mostly slip under the per-thread untracked-memory threshold,
  which is why it looked sporadic rather than like an outage.

  WHAT THIS SETS

  1. memory_worker_correct_memory_tracker = 1. The background memory worker
     re-baselines the global tracker from a measurement of the memory the
     process really uses, on every tick (tens of milliseconds), so drift cannot
     accumulate. Upstream: ClickHouse PR #75714 (25.1, backported to 24.11 and
     24.12) added it OFF by default; PR #118094 turns it on by default from
     26.10. 24.10 does not have it at all — hence the image pin in compose.

  2. max_server_memory_usage_to_ram_ratio = 0.9, stated rather than assumed.
     compose once set CLICKHOUSE_MAX_SERVER_MEMORY_USAGE_TO_RAM_RATIO=0.6, but
     the official image's entrypoint reads no such variable: every install ran
     at ClickHouse's own default of 0.9 of the container limit. 0.9 is what the
     deployments have been proven on, so it is kept — and now it is written
     where ClickHouse actually reads it. With the tracker corrected from real
     usage, the limit now means what it says.

  CHECKING IT

      docker exec sentinel-clickhouse-1 clickhouse-client -q "
        SELECT name, value FROM system.server_settings
        WHERE name IN ('memory_worker_correct_memory_tracker',
                       'max_server_memory_usage_to_ram_ratio')"

  and compare system.metrics MemoryTracking with the asynchronous metric
  MemoryResident: with the correction on they stay within a few hundred MiB.
  verify.sh reports both.
-->
<clickhouse>
    <memory_worker_correct_memory_tracker>1</memory_worker_correct_memory_tracker>
    <max_server_memory_usage_to_ram_ratio>0.9</max_server_memory_usage_to_ram_ratio>
</clickhouse>
